==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|pudenda||that where of one ought to feel shame|lang=la}}, substantive use of the neuter plural [[gerundive]] of {{term|pudet||it shames|lang=la}}; in Latin the usage in the plural form (to mean external [[genitalia]]), was far more common than the singular form, as is also the case in English.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{enPR|pyo͝odĕnʹdəm|pyo͞odĕnʹdəm}}, {{IPA|/pjʊˈdɛndəm/|/pjuːˈdɛndəm/|lang=en}}
* {{a|RP}} {{enPR|pyo͞odĕnʹdəm}}, {{IPA|/pjuˈdɛndəm/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|pudenda}}

# {{context|usually in the plural|lang=en}} An external [[genital]] organ in a [[human]]; especially a woman’s [[vulva]].
# {{context|figuratively|lang=en}} A [[shameful]] part of [[something]].

====Usage notes====
* This term appears far more frequently in the plural — as {{term|pudenda|lang=en}} — than in the singular, [[analogous]]ly with {{term|genitalia|lang=en}}, which is rarely encountered in its obscure singular form {{term|genitale|lang=en}}, and with {{term|genitals|lang=en}}, a ''[[plurale tantum]]'' whose supposed singular form {{term|genital|lang=en}} is an adjective in English.

